          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"skram-constance-ring-eff8f54f2ce7f7bd\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Lorck’s retrospective “jeg elskede dig” (“I loved you”) straightforwardly presents his past love for Constance as true. It supports reassurance within their marriage, but does not itself undertake or renew a commitment. Nothing indicates that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge rather than a deliberate autobiographical avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"Lorck may be idealising his past and seeking reciprocal affirmation, but possible self-serving framing or insincerity would not alter the utterance’s strongly truth-conditional mode.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Lorck directly tells Constance that he loved her, repeating the retrospective avowal while recounting his perseverance before and during their marriage.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"In an intimate marital conversation, Lorck casts Constance as the first person who taught him genuine love and contrasts that love with his earlier affairs. The avowal reassures her and supports his idealised account of their relationship; he subsequently presses her to confirm that her life likewise begins with him. These relational and potentially self-serving effects remain contextual rather than constituting a commitment encoded by the core utterance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“men som sagt, jeg elskede dig, jeg har aldrig elsket, før jeg begyndte at elske dig” (“but, as I said, I loved you; I had never loved before I began to love you”).\",\"supports\":\"The explicit retrospective avowal and comparison with his prior life strongly support truth-conditional force.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Lorck introduces the avowal while describing how persistently he pursued Constance despite her discouragement.\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is a considered explanation of his past conduct, not a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Constance asks, “Er det virkelig sandt, Nils, sig, er det sandt?” (“Is that really true, Nils? Tell me, is it true?”).\",\"supports\":\"Her response explicitly treats Lorck’s words as a claim whose truth can be questioned and confirmed.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked affirmative, past tense/aspect, and a valid explicit love-I-you instance.\",\"supports\":\"The metadata corroborates identification of the core event as a past-directed love avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T alone naturally captures the core force. The marital reassurance, retrospective idealisation, and pressure for reciprocity are contextual effects adequately described outside the compact ontology.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
